  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">DFAT has partnered with the United Nations Office on Crime and Drugs (UNODC) to support interagency cooperation within the Philippine criminal justice system to respond to terrorism. Through technical assistance, Australia supported the development of the standard operating procedures (SOPs) for the Department of Justice&#x2019;s Circular 20, which mandates prosecutors to work closely with law enforcement agencies and investigate terrorism and terrorism financing cases.  
&#x200d;
This has institutionalised cooperation between six Philippine law and justice agencies in the   investigation and prosecution of terrorism cases s. It also ensures that these efforts accord with international standards and reinforce existing safeguards that protect the rights of individuals accused of terrorism. Australia&#x2019;s investment supports the Philippine&#x2019;s law and justice sector to enable proactive investigations and produce fair trials in terrorism and violent extremism cases.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">DFAT&#x2019;s partnership with The Asia Foundation has strengthened the administration of justice in violent extremism cases in the Philippines.  
&#x200d;
Through technical assistance, Australia supported the development of the first training curriculum for the Supreme Court of the Philippines on terrorism-related legislation. The curriculum comprises 16 modules on various aspects of adjudicating terrorism and terrorism financing cases, including a focus on international standards, human rights and effective judicial protections. Australia also supported the creation of a court manual to assist judges in handling terrorism cases.  
&#x200d;
The Supreme Court has adopted and institutionalised these reforms. A training program has been developed to roll-out these initiatives and 259 judges have since received the training. The 16 modules are now part of the Supreme Court&#x2019;s national training curriculum through the Philippine Judicial Academy.  
&#x200d;
This project has led to an increased understanding of terrorism legislation within the Philippine Judiciary and strengthened organisational policies and practices in the handling of violent extremism and terrorism cases, including human rights perspectives and protections. The project has increased transparency and accountability within the judicial system.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">DFAT&#x2019;s partnership with the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime brings together criminal justice stakeholders to improve prison management in the Philippines, particularly when it comes to managing violent extremist offenders. Australia funded technical assistance, equipment, and training on prison management based on international standards for the Bureau of Corrections and the Bureau of Jail Management and Penology. The investment led to a standardised system for the intake and classification of individuals, including violent extremist offenders. This system is designed to detect risks, including radicalisation levels of each individual, and identify the rehabilitation and support required to reintegrate them back to society successfully.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
